  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nHere's the problem - when you use `delegatecall` in multicall, every operation runs with the Covenant contract's full authority. The market-level locks are solid, but they don't cover the gaps between different operations in the same multicall batch.\n\nThink about it this way: an attacker could slip a malicious call into the batch that calls back into the contract before the next operation executes. This could let them manipulate state in between legitimate operations, potentially messing with fee calculations or market states.\n\nWhat worries me is that while each individual market operation is locked down, the multicall wrapper itself isn't guarded. It's like having a secure building where each room has a lock, but the hallway connecting them doesn't.\n\nThe damage could range from minor state inconsistencies to someone gaming the system for financial advantage. It's one of those \"shouldn't happen\" things that becomes a real headache when it does.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nHonestly, the fix is straightforward - just slap a reentrancy guard on the multicall function itself:\n```solidity\nbool private _multicallLock;\n\nmodifier multicallLock() {\n    require(!_multicallLock, \"No reentrancy\");\n    _multicallLock = true;\n    _;\n    _multicallLock = false;\n}\n\nfunction multicall(bytes[] calldata data) external payable multicallLock returns (bytes[] memory results) {\n    // existing code\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, you could switch from `delegatecall` to regular external calls to isolate the execution contexts. Either way, this is one of those \"better safe than sorry\" fixes that's cheap to implement and saves potential headaches down the road.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport \"../src/Covenant.sol\";\n\ncontract MulticallReentrancyTest is Test {\n    Covenant covenant;\n    \n    function setUp() public {\n        covenant = new Covenant(address(this));\n    }\n    \n    function testMulticallReentrancy() public {\n        ReentrancyExploiter exploiter = new ReentrancyExploiter(address(covenant));\n        \n        bytes[] memory calls = new bytes[](2);\n        calls[0] = abi.encodeWithSignature(\"triggerReentrancy()\");\n        calls[1] = abi.encodeWithSignature(\"normalOperation()\");\n        \n        // This should blow up if there's proper protection\n        vm.expectRevert();\n        exploiter.executeAttack(calls);\n    }\n}\n\ncontract ReentrancyExploiter {\n    Covenant covenant;\n    bool private alreadyReentered;\n    \n    constructor(address _covenant) {\n        covenant = Covenant(_covenant);\n    }\n    \n    function triggerReentrancy() external {\n        if (!alreadyReentered) {\n            alreadyReentered = true;\n            // Try to jump back in during the multicall\n            bytes[] memory secondCall = new bytes[](1);\n            secondCall[0] = abi.encodeWithSignature(\"updateState(...)\");\n            covenant.multicall(secondCall);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    function executeAttack(bytes[] memory calls) external {\n        covenant.multicall(calls);\n    }\n}\n```",
